FINAL ANSWER:
**Bus factor is 2 for the whole repo: Troy Goode and Douglas Christopher Wilson.** That number is line-based. It is the smallest number of authors whose surviving lines reach 50% of the lines counted. Scope: whole repo, 1,662 non-blank lines (1,995 with blank lines), 18 files, `git blame -w -M -C`. To reach 80% you need 4 authors.

**Whole repo, by surviving lines**

| Author | Lines | Share | Commits |
|---|---|---|---|
| Troy Goode (2 addresses) | 649 | 39.0% | 95 (30.2%) |
| Douglas Christopher Wilson | 551 | 33.2% | 143 (45.4%) |
| Íñigo Marquínez Prado | 80 | 4.8% | 2 |
| Jon Church | 59 | 3.5% | 3 |
| Sebastian Beltran | 57 | 3.4% | 2 |
| Ulises Gascón | 46 | 2.8% | 3 |
| Ignacio Mangas | 38 | 2.3% | 2 |
| Alejandro Caravaca Puchades | 30 | 1.8% | 5 |
| Linus Unnebäck | 26 | 1.6% | 1 |
| Ollie Buck | 14 | 0.8% | 1 |

52 more authors hold 6.7% together. The largest of them is Eric di Domenico at 0.7%.

**Single-contributor areas (per directory)**

| Directory | Bus factor | Top holder | Scope |
|---|---|---|---|
| `lib/` | 1 | Troy Goode, 144 of 214 lines (67.3%) | 1 file (`lib/index.js`) |
| `test/` | 1 | Troy Goode, 420 of 820 lines (51.2%) | 7 files |
| root (`.`) | 2 | Wilson 29.4%, Troy 26.3% | 6 files, 323 lines |
| `.github/` | 2 | Wilson 30.2%, Íñigo Marquínez Prado 26.2% | 4 files, 305 lines |

- **Troy Goode is the person the code depends on.** He holds two-thirds of the main source file and about half of the tests. Wilson is second in both places (9.3% of `lib/`, 42.0% of `test/`).
- **In `lib/`, Troy's share is the merged figure.** `lib/` is the only directory where Wilson is clearly not a co-owner. Merged (2 addresses), Troy holds 144 lines. His `<email redacted>` address alone has fewer.
- **Wilson's last commit is 2021-09-07.** Troy's is 2026-04-10. Wilson's 33% of the code has had no recent maintainer, which is a risk in its own right.
- **`.github/` is spread out.** It has five holders and no one above 30%. Even so, two people cover 56%.

**Caveats**
- **Same-person merges are guesses.** Troy Goode is counted as one person across `<email redacted>` (605 lines, 93 commits) and `troy@laptop.example` (44 lines, 2 commits). It rests on the same name and is not confirmed. Unmerged, `<email redacted>` alone holds 605 lines (36.4%), and he is still the top author. The `.github/` and root tables were computed with the same merge. In the root table only one of Troy's addresses appears.
- **One address, several names.** `<email redacted>` appears as both "Alejandro Caravaca Puchades" and "alex94puchades". The tool counts them as one person with 30 lines. In `lib/` the table shows the `alex94puchades` name, with 14 lines (6.5%). I did not write a `.mailmap`, since you asked only for analysis.
- **Reformat commit ignored.** `62628c0` is "style: reformat js with 4-space indent and double quotes" by Fiona Formatter. It rewrote 1,016 lines across 4 files (`lib/index.js` and three test files), and I confirmed that with `git show --stat`. The tables leave it out. With it counted, Fiona Formatter would hold 291 lines (17.5%), Wilson 376 (22.6%) and Troy 554 (33.3%). The bus factor stays 2, but the two together would hold only 56.0%.
- **Mechanical rewrites checked.** The tool examined 136 commits with surviving lines and set aside 1 (`62628c0`). It flagged 5 more as looking mechanical and kept them:
  - `739dc11` (docs: reformat license): 17 lines
  - `fcd2b56` (issue #42: switch to ESLint): 11 lines
  - `00d6eec` (build: use yaml eslint configuration): 9 lines
  - `344ce1e` (build: <email redacted>): 1 line
  - `9c2b59d` (build: <email redacted>): 1 line

  They total 39 lines, so they change nothing material.
- **Bots excluded.** `dependabot[bot]`: 10 lines and 11 commits, not credited.
- **Copy detection.** The figures use the default `-C`. The tool reported no copy-detection note for any author.
- **Small sample.** The repo has only 18 files and about 1.7k lines, so one file (`lib/index.js`) drives much of the result.
- **What the numbers don't show.** They don't measure code quality, review, design work or code deleted on purpose. A high share of surviving lines does not mean the person understands the code best.
